::ENG4_U03_L02_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: Community repair cafes can reduce waste while helping residents learn practical skills. Volunteers help people fix items, and one cafe reported that residents repaired 68 percent of items brought in over six months. What is the central idea? {
~A. Repair cafes are only social gatherings.#This misses waste reduction and skill learning.
=B. Repair cafes can reduce waste and teach useful repair skills.#Correct. This captures the full central idea.
~C. Residents should throw away broken items immediately.#This contradicts the passage.
~D. Volunteers cannot help with practical skills.#This contradicts the passage.
}
::ENG4_U03_L02_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: One repair cafe reported that residents repaired 68 percent of items brought in over six months. Why is this effective support? {
=A. It gives specific measurable evidence connected to the central idea.#Correct. The statistic directly supports the claim.
~B. It is unrelated to repair cafes.#It directly relates to repair cafes.
~C. It is vague and unsupported.#The percentage is specific.
~D. It proves every item can be repaired.#It says 68 percent, not every item.
}
::ENG4_U03_L02_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: A passage first states a central idea, then gives examples, then evaluates the strongest evidence. Why is this structure effective? {
~A. It hides the main idea until the end.#The main idea appears first.
~B. It avoids evidence.#It gives examples and evaluates evidence.
=C. It helps readers understand the claim before judging support.#Correct. The order supports comprehension.
~D. It turns the passage into fiction.#The structure is informational.
}
::ENG4_U03_L02_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Participants said they were more likely to try simple repairs at home after watching volunteers explain each step. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Participants became less willing to repair anything.#This reverses the meaning.
~B. Volunteers refused to explain repairs.#This contradicts the passage.
~C. Participants only watched and learned nothing.#The passage says they were more likely to try repairs.
=D. Watching volunteers made participants more willing to attempt simple repairs themselves.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
}
::ENG4_U03_L02_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The evidence is sufficient because it gives enough information to support the central idea. What does sufficient mean? {
=A. Enough for the purpose#Correct. Sufficient means adequate or enough.
~B. Unrelated#This is the opposite.
~C. Decorative#This is unrelated.
~D. Impossible to understand#This is not the meaning.
}
